<article class="env-definition" id="019N">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/019N">Definition <span data-tag="019N">14.26.6</span>.</a> Let $U$ and $V$ be two simplicial objects of a category $\mathcal{C}$. We say a morphism $a : U \to V$ is a <em>homotopy equivalence</em> if there exists a morphism $b : V \to U$ such that $a \circ b$ is homotopic to $\text{id}_ V$ and $b \circ a$ is homotopic to $\text{id}_ U$. We say $U$ and $V$ are <em>homotopy equivalent</em> if there exists a homotopy equivalence $a : U \to V$. </p>
</article>